Q: Show &#39;space&#39; character via listchars only for leading spaces A: <p>This is a solution for neovim from 2024.</p>
<p>Inspired by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/40498439/">rodrigo's answer</a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-lua prettyprint-override"><code>vim.cmd([[2match WhiteSpaceBol /^ \+/]])
vim.cmd('match WhiteSpaceMol / /')
vim.api.nvim_set_hl(0, 'WhiteSpaceMol', {
    fg = string.format('#%x', vim.api.nvim_get_hl(0, { name = 'Normal' }).bg)
})
</code></pre>
<p>When switching between coloschemes, it's not suffice. So register a autocmd to handel it:</p>
<pre class="lang-lua prettyprint-override"><code>vim.cmd([[2match WhiteSpaceBol /^ \+/]])
vim.cmd('match WhiteSpaceMol / /')
vim.api.nvim_set_hl(0, 'WhiteSpaceMol', {
    fg = string.format('#%x', vim.api.nvim_get_hl(0, { name = 'Normal' }).bg)
})
vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('ColorScheme', {
    callback = function()
        vim.api.nvim_set_hl(0, 'WhiteSpaceMol', {
            fg = string.format('#%x', vim.api.nvim_get_hl(0, { name = 'Normal' }).bg)
        })
    end
})

</code></pre>
<p>The only issue here is the space will still distinct if cursorline has different color from <code>Normal</code>.</p>

Q: Loading variables into JSON A: <p>all I had to do is converting the JSON into string and then reconvert it to JSON</p>
<pre><code>${original_payload}=    Get File    json.json
${payload}=    Replace Variables    ${original_payload}
${payload_with_variables}=    Convert String To Json    ${payload}
</code></pre>
<p>Then I use the last payload as body on my POST call</p>

Q: Changing font family of all Material UI components A: <p>I am using Next.js with MUI and Tailwind.</p>
<p>My setup is:</p>
<p>AppStylesProvider - my wrapper around styles</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>export const AppStylesProvider = ({
  children,
}: AppStylesThemeProviderProps) =&gt; {
  const theme = createMuiAppTheme()

  return (
    &lt;AppRouterCacheProvider
      options={{
        enableCssLayer: true,
      }}
    &gt;
      &lt;ThemeProvider theme={theme}&gt;{children}&lt;/ThemeProvider&gt;
    &lt;/AppRouterCacheProvider&gt;
  )
}

// createMuiAppTheme.ts - helper function
export const createMuiAppTheme = () =&gt; {
  const baseTokens: ThemeOptions = {
    modularCssLayers: '@layer theme, base, mui, components, utilities',
    cssVariables: true,
    ...
  }

const baseComponents: ThemeOptions = {
  typography: {
    fontFamily: 'var(--font-primary)',
  }
  components: {
    ...
  }

const baseThemeOptions: ThemeOptions = {
    ...baseTokens,
    ...baseComponents,
}

return createTheme(baseThemeOptions)
}
</code></pre>
<p>Tailwind.css</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@theme {
  --font-primary: var(--font-saira), Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;
}
</code></pre>
<p>layout.ts</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { Saira } from 'next/font/google'
import { AppStylesProvider } from 'path-to.../AppStylesProvider'
import 'tailwind.css'
...

const saira = Saira({
  subsets: ['latin'],
  display: 'swap',
  variable: '--font-saira',
})

export default async function MainLayout({
  children,
  params,
}: {
  children: ReactNode
  params: Promise&lt;{ locale: string }&gt;
}) {
&lt;html className={saira.variable}&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;AppStylesProvider&gt;
      {children}
    &lt;/AppStylesProvider&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
}
</code></pre>
<p>I hope this helps someone.</p>

Q: something like regex style textbox validation in css A: <p>Standard CSS does not support a regex pseudo-class. You can create &quot;regex-like&quot; behavior for validation using CSS Attribute Selectors and the invalid pseudo-class.</p>
<ul>
<li>You can use the pattern attribute in HTML to define a regex. CSS then styles the input based on whether the content matches that pattern, using the valid and invalid pseudo-classes.</li>
</ul>
<p>HTML:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; pattern=&quot;[0-9]*&quot; class=&quot;regex-input&quot; placeholder=&quot;Numbers only&quot;&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>CSS:</p>
<pre><code>/* If the regex does not match */
.regex-input:invalid {
  border: 2px solid red;
  color: red;
}

/* If the regex matches */
.regex-input:valid {
  border: 2px solid blue;
  color: blue;
}
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li>While still not true regex, the new <code>has()</code> selector lets you style an element based on its children or state. For example, you can hide a message only when an input is valid.</li>
</ul>
<p>CSS:</p>
<pre><code>/* Hide the error message if the input is valid */
.container:has(input:valid) #error_message {
  display: none;
}
</code></pre>
